{
  "_comment": "Translation mapping for POST /api/vivaha-pontana. Every key, enumerated value and piece of text the endpoint can output is covered; TestTranslationsCoverEveryOutputString (internal/astrology/matchmaking) fails if the backend can emit anything that is missing here. English is the backend's exact output; te is Telugu.",
  "_usage": [
    "1. JSON key names: look them up in `fields` (en/te label). The 8 koota keys under ashtakoota.kootas use `kootas`, the 7 keys under checks use `checks`, the 4 keys under groom.risks / bride.risks use `risks`.",
    "2. Enumerated values (signs, nakshatras, planets, yoni animals, ganas, varnas, nadis, rajju groups, taras, relationships, qualities): `enum_paths` says which `enums` set translates the value found at a JSON path ('*' stands for groom or bride). Look the value up in enums.<set>.<value>.te.",
    "3. Free text (every `explanation`, `cancellation_reason`, `suggestion` and each item of `risk_factors`): first look the exact English text up in `strings`. If it is not there, match it against `templates`: turn the template into a regular expression by escaping it and replacing each {param} with (.+?), anchored at both ends; the first template whose parameters are all valid wins. Translate each captured value with the enum named in the template's `params` (null = copy the value unchanged, it is a number), then put them into the template's `te` text.",
    "4. Numbers, scores, booleans and the bhakoot `relationship` distance string (for example 10/4) are shown as they are. Person names (groom.name, bride.name) are user input and are never translated.",
    "5. The `name` field of a koota or check equals kootas.<key>.en / checks.<key>.en; prefer translating by the JSON key."
  ],
